# TicketHub: System Design

TicketHub is a website that sells tickets for concerts and events. This document follows the six-part framework: requirements, estimates, API, data model, architecture and trade-offs. A dedicated section explains how the design prevents two people from buying the same seat.

Facts used: 2 million registered users; 50,000 people visit on a normal day, each viewing 10 pages; 5,000 tickets are sold on a normal day; when a popular concert goes on sale, 200,000 people try to buy its 20,000 seats within the first 10 minutes.

---

## 1. Requirements

### Functional
- Users can register, log in and log out.
- Users can browse and search events (by city, date, artist).
- Users can view an event and its seat map with live availability.
- Users can **hold** one or more seats for a short time (5 minutes) while they pay.
- Users can pay for held seats and receive a confirmation and tickets (with a QR code).
- Users can view their orders and tickets.
- Held seats that are not paid for are released automatically.

### Non-functional
- **Correctness (most important):** a seat is never sold to two people, and no one is charged without receiving a ticket. This beats speed whenever the two conflict.
- **Fairness:** during a big sale, people are served roughly in the order they arrived; bots and refresh-spamming should not win.
- **Speed:** browsing pages load in under 300 ms (p95); seat hold and payment respond in under 1 second (p95), excluding the payment provider.
- **Availability:** 99.9% uptime overall, and the site must stay up (not crash) during a big sale even if some people have to wait.
- **Scalability:** handle a sudden spike of hundreds of times normal traffic.
- **Durability:** a paid order is never lost (replicated database, backups).
- **Security:** HTTPS, hashed passwords, no card numbers stored (the payment provider handles cards), rate limiting.

---

## 2. Estimates

### Normal day

| Metric | Calculation | Result |
|--------|-------------|--------|
| Page views per day | 50,000 visitors x 10 pages | 500,000 |
| Average page views per second | 500,000 / 86,400 | about 6/s |
| Peak page views per second (10x average, evening rush) | 6 x 10 | about 60/s |
| Tickets sold per day | given | 5,000 |
| Average purchases per second | 5,000 / 86,400 | about 0.06/s (one every 17 seconds) |
| Storage per year | 5,000 x 365 = 1.8 million tickets x about 1 KB | about 2 GB/year |

### Big sale (one popular concert)

| Metric | Calculation | Result |
|--------|-------------|--------|
| Users arriving | 200,000 in 10 minutes (600 s) | about 333 new users/s |
| Page and seat-map requests | assume 10 requests per user in 10 minutes: 2,000,000 / 600 | about 3,300 requests/s |
| Seat hold attempts | assume 2 attempts per user: 400,000 / 600 | about 670/s |
| Successful sales | 20,000 seats / 600 s | about 33 orders/s |
| Demand vs supply | 200,000 users / 20,000 seats | 10 users for every seat |

### Comparison

| | Normal (peak hour) | Big sale | Increase |
|---|---|---|---|
| Read requests per second | about 60 | about 3,300 | about 55x (about 550x vs the daily average) |
| Purchases per second | about 0.06 (average) | about 33 | about 500x |
| Seat hold attempts | negligible | about 670/s, 95% of which must fail fast | n/a |

Two lessons: (1) the spike is hundreds of times the normal load, so we cannot just size the system for a normal day; (2) 20,000 sales in 10 minutes equals four normal days of sales, so the write path (holding and paying) is the part that must be protected most.

